ParticleOS Stalwart provisioning
================================

The image owns Stalwart's database dependency. On first boot it initializes a
checksummed PostgreSQL cluster in /var/lib/pgsql, disables every TCP listener,
creates the unprivileged stalwart database role, and authenticates the
stalwart OS account through PostgreSQL peer authentication on
/run/postgresql. There is no database password or environment file.

PostgreSQL, role provisioning, and Stalwart are enabled by the image. Complete
application bootstrap as follows:

1. Connect through the console or an SSH tunnel to localhost:8080. Port 8080
   is intentionally never admitted by the public firewall.
2. Create the permanent administrator and configure the production hostname,
   TLS certificates, Ed25519 DKIM keys, domains, relay policy, and abuse
   controls before accepting production mail.
3. Keep the local HTTP bootstrap listener available on loopback: the counted
   update-health gate uses /admin to verify the OBS-packaged WebUI before
   blessing a new OS slot.

The public firewall admits only TCP 25, 443, 465, and 993. POP3, ManageSieve,
plaintext client protocols, PostgreSQL, and bootstrap TCP 8080 are closed.
Stalwart may create external connections only to SMTP TCP 25 and HTTPS TCP 443.
DNS packets are limited to loopback and therefore must pass through
systemd-resolved. resolved authenticates Cloudflare over DNS-over-TLS and
performs strict local DNSSEC validation; blocked DoT or failed validation makes
mail resolution fail closed.

The WebUI is a checksum-pinned RPM payload in immutable /usr. Stalwart rejects
every application-bundle URL except file:///usr/share/stalwart/webui.zip, so
the WebUI cannot update outside the signed OBS and system-update supply chain.

The particleos_stalwart SELinux module confines the service to its labelled
configuration, WebUI, state, logs, runtime and private temporary files; the
PostgreSQL Unix socket; the system resolver; and the selected SMTP, IMAP and
HTTP port types. Diagnose the mail stack with:

    run0 systemctl status postgresql.service particleos-stalwart-database.service stalwart.service
    run0 systemctl start particleos-mailserver-health.service
    run0 journalctl -u particleos-mailserver-health.service
    run0 -u stalwart psql --host=/run/postgresql --dbname=stalwart --command='SELECT 1'

Changing to a remote database, adding protocols, or removing the loopback
bootstrap listener changes the appliance security and health model. Do that
only through a reviewed signed image change covering nftables, SELinux,
credentials, service ordering, health checks, and documentation.
